  note         = {Cited for the rule that Congress cannot enlarge the Supreme Court’s original jurisdiction beyond the cases Article III, \S\,2, assigns to it, so that an original action outside those cases must begin in an inferior court. Marshall, C.J., for the Court. At 174: “If congress remains at liberty to give this court appellate jurisdiction, where the constitution has declared their jurisdiction shall be original; and original jurisdiction where the constitution has declared it shall be appellate; the distribution of jurisdiction, made in the constitution, is form without substance,” and “Affirmative words are often, in their operation, negative of other objects than those affirmed.” At 176: the authority given by the Judiciary Act to issue writs of mandamus to public officers “appears not to be warranted by the constitution.” Agent-verified 2026-09-26 against the Cornell LII text of the opinion and the Library of Congress scan of 5 U.S. 137, from which the pin pages are counted (page 174 holds both quoted sentences on original jurisdiction; page 176 the mandamus conclusion). Not checked: whether any later case qualifies the rule in a way bearing on the manuscript’s proposal.},
